After decreasing the defining $p_{10}$ radius of $\mathcal U$, each $d\in\mathcal U$ has a smooth globally hyperbolic vacuum comparison development $\mathcal P_d$ containing the whole bridge $\Sigma$ as a Cauchy hypersurface and embedded openly and isometrically in $\mathcal M_d$. It contains a spacelike cylinder $\mathcal S$ and its full future product wedge, with coordinates $t=(u+v)/2\ge0$, $x=(v-u)/2\in\mathbb R$, $\theta\in\mathbb S^2$, in which $$\label{test:wedge}
 \begin{split}
 g&=-2a\,du\,dv+
 \gamma_{AB}(d\theta^A-b^Adu)(d\theta^B-b^Bdu),\\
 a&=q\mathcal A,\qquad
 q=q_0e^{-\kappa(u+v)},\qquad q_0,\kappa>0.
 \end{split}$$ The coefficients $\mathcal A^{\pm1}$ and $\gamma^{\pm1}$ are uniformly bounded, with $\gamma$ uniformly comparable to a fixed round sphere metric. Every seed with $T_d(w)<\infty$ crosses $\mathcal S$ before its first runout from $\mathcal P_d$, and that first runout has exactly one of the following coordinate limits: $$\label{test:runout}
 \begin{array}{lll}
 \text{first branch:}&u\to u_*<\infty,&v\to+\infty,\\
 \text{second branch:}&v\to v_*<\infty,&u\to+\infty,\\
 \text{corner:}&u\to+\infty,&v\to+\infty.
 \end{array}$$ These alternatives describe limiting coordinate regimes; they do not adjoin boundary points or identify $\mathcal P_d$ with $\mathcal M_d$.

There is a choice of $\epsilon>0$, depending only on the fixed Kerr center and the fixed preparation, such that every $p_{10}(d-d_*)<\epsilon$ in the stated smooth symbol class has the following properties.*


1.  *The cylinder $\mathcal S$ in Proposition <a href="#test:comparison" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="test:comparison">12</a> has a smooth vacuum development on the full product wedge $[0,\infty)_t\times\mathbb R_x\times\mathbb S^2$ in Equation <a href="#bg:wedge" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="bg:wedge">[bg:wedge]</a>. The quantities $\mathcal A^{\pm1},\gamma^{\pm1}$ are uniformly bounded in angular $H^6$, and $\xi_i,a^{-1}\partial_vb$ in angular $H^5$. There are nonnegative functions $g_i(y_i)$ with summable unit-cell suprema such that, in the indicated low angular norms, $$\label{bg:envelopes}
     \|\chi_i,\ell_i+\kappa\|_{H^4}\le C(q+g_i(y_i)),
     \qquad \|b\|_{H^5}\le C(q+g_0(u)).$$*


2.  *The following quantities are uniformly bounded, and tend to zero when $t,|u|,|v|\to\infty$: $$\label{bg:low-limits}
    \begin{gathered}
     \|b\|_{H^5}+\sum_i\|\chi_i,\ell_i+\kappa\|_{H^4},\\
     \sum_{k=0}^1\left(\|\partial_{y_k}b\|_{H^2}
           +\sum_i\|\partial_{y_k}\chi_i,
                              \partial_{y_k}\ell_i\|_{H^2}\right),\\
     \sum_{k=0}^1\left(\|\partial_{y_k}\gamma\|_{H^4}
                     +\|\partial_{y_k}\log a+\kappa\|_{H^4}\right).
    \end{gathered}$$ In particular $D\log a,D\gamma,b,Db$ are uniformly bounded.*



3.  *For each fixed $U\in\mathbb R$, $C>0$, and finite order, all ordinary derivatives of $$\label{bg:high-arrays}
     \gamma^{\pm1},\quad\log\mathcal A,\quad b,
                      \quad a^{-1}\partial_vb$$ are bounded by $e^{o(T)}$ on $$\label{bg:truncation}
     t\ge0,\qquad y_d\le U,\qquad y_l\le CT,
     \qquad \{d,l\}=\{0,1\}.$$ This includes every restriction and coordinate-path subsegment. Every fixed derivative of $a$, $\sqrt a$, or $\partial_vb$ retains, respectively, the factor $a$, $\sqrt a$, or $a$.*

4.  *Either branch can be put in the convention $v\to\infty$, $u\to u_*<\infty$. Exchanging the optical labels and preparing the angular coordinates anew preserves all these conclusions on the same product spacetime and initial cylinder.*

*Only $p_{10}$ is required small; no higher seminorm, radiation coefficient, or curvature measured by a timelike observer is required small or bounded.

Let a future unit timelike geodesic approach a branch $v\to\infty$, $u\to u_*<\infty$, in the comparison wedge. With proper time denoted by $\tau$, put $$p=a\dot v,\qquad \Xi=\dot\theta-b\dot u,\qquad P=\gamma\Xi.$$ Then $p\asymp1$, and $P$, $\Xi$, and $\dot u$ stay bounded on a late segment. The angular position has a limit. At the point $p_T$ with $v=T$ one has $$\label{bg:branch-scales}
             a_p:=a(p_T)\asymp e^{-\kappa T},
             \qquad \dot v(p_T)\asymp e^{\kappa T}.$$

Let $V_\alpha$ be the matrix of $\nabla_{Z_\alpha}$ on the normalized vector basis. These matrices are bounded, and in the bulk limit $\min(v,-u,t)\to\infty$, $$\label{bg:connection-limits}
 V_0\longrightarrow D_0=\operatorname{diag}(-\kappa/2,\kappa/2,0,0),
 \qquad V_1\longrightarrow-D_0,\qquad V_A\longrightarrow0.$$ The differentiated connection terms contracted in the tensor operator $a\square_g$, and every normalized entry of $a\operatorname{Riem}(g)$, are bounded and tend to zero in this limit. Their fixed ordinary jets and those of the brackets of $Z$ are $e^{o(T)}$ on Equation <a href="#bg:truncation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="bg:truncation">[bg:truncation]</a>. Moreover $\nabla_{L_1}L_1=\ell_1L_1$ exactly. The undifferentiated constant connection products are retained: on entry $n$ the limiting radial operator is $$\label{bg:radial-conjugation}
\begin{split}
 &-(L_0+c_n)(L_1-c_n)-(L_1-c_n)(L_0+c_n)\\
 &\qquad=-L_0L_1-L_1L_0+2c_n(L_0-L_1)+2c_n^2.
\end{split}$$ It is the radial part of $e^{2c_nx}a\square_g^{\rm scal}e^{-2c_nx}$.

Fix any finite list of output derivative orders, including an exterior base buffer $N_b$. For every fixed $\zeta>0$ and every subsequently prescribed inverse accuracy $M_1$, sufficiently high finite transport and Taylor orders yield a real interior tensor $h_T^p$ and a real exterior tensor $h_T^B$ with the following properties. Put $$\label{bg:packet-scales}
       A_T=e^{-\alpha T},\qquad \alpha=7\kappa/8,
       \qquad\lambda_T=e^{\zeta T}.$$ The interior field has the form $$\label{bg:packet-form}
 h_T^p=A_T\operatorname{tr.rev.}_g\operatorname{Re}
       \left(e^{i\lambda_Tv}\sum_{j=0}^{J}\lambda_T^{-j}p_j\right).$$ Every fixed normalized coefficient jet of $p_j$ is $e^{o(T)}$. The field is supported in a fixed-width neighborhood of $v=T$, with angular support in the transported launch patch. Its relative array $q_T=(h_T^p(\hat e_\alpha,\hat e_\beta))$ satisfies $$\label{bg:packet-size}
              \|q_T\|_{C^N(\mathcal D_T)}
                    \le A_T\lambda_T^N e^{o(T)}$$ at each specified order. The tensor $p_0$ is trace-free and $p_0(L_0,\cdot)=0$. There is a purely angular $\gamma$-unit vector $X$ at $p_T$ for which $$\label{bg:polarization}
 \sigma_T:=\left|\operatorname{Re}
        \bigl(e^{i\lambda_TT}p_0(X,X)\bigr)\right|$$ has two-sided subexponential size. The $a$-scaled linear wave residual and $\sqrt a$-scaled linear gauge residual, in normalized slots and with all prescribed ordinary derivatives, are bounded by $A_T\lambda_T^{-M_1}e^{o(T)}$.*


*In regular exterior lab components, $$\label{bg:beam-estimates}
 \|h_T^B\|_{C^{N_b}}\le A_T\lambda_T^{N_b+3/2}e^{o(T)}.$$ Its linear wave and gauge residuals, and its difference from $h_T^p$ on the entry collar, have size $A_T\lambda_T^{-M_1}e^{o(T)}$ at all the prescribed finite orders. The latter assertion includes normal spacetime jets. On the original bridge its support lies in Equation <a href="#bg:arrival-shell" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="bg:arrival-shell">[bg:arrival-shell]</a> with arbitrarily thin tube margins. Increasing $M_1$ and the finite formal degrees does not increase the positive frequency powers in Equations <a href="#bg:packet-size" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="bg:packet-size">[bg:packet-size]</a> and <a href="#bg:beam-estimates" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="bg:beam-estimates">[bg:beam-estimates]</a>. Thresholds and subexponential constants may depend on those degrees.

Fix a finite data order $m\ge10$, a finite entry order $r$, a loss $\eta>0$, and $0<\nu_0<1$. After fixing all the finite derivative orders needed below, one can choose $\zeta>0$ and then the finite formal accuracy orders in Proposition <a href="#bg:packet" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="bg:packet">21</a> so that the following hold for all sufficiently large parameters $T$ in the packet sequence.*


*There are smooth positive complete vacuum data $d_T^\pm\in\mathcal D$ on the whole bridge, with $$\label{prep:data-convergence}
 p_m(d_T^\pm-d)\longrightarrow0.$$ In particular $d_T^\pm\in\mathcal U$ for large $T$. They agree exactly with $d$ on the other end, the connecting region, and a protected region containing every fixed compact subset for large $T$. On the selected asymptotic end the protected ball is $$\label{prep:protected-ball}
 B_T=\{|z-z_*|<\rho_T\},\qquad
 z_*=C_e(t_T),\qquad \rho_T=(1-2\beta)t_T,$$ where $\beta>0$ is fixed sufficiently small. The data have the weight-one metric and weight-two second-form symbol bounds at every order. Their full correction tails are retained; asymptotic coefficients are allowed to change.*


*Ordinary exterior vacuum evolutions $g_T^\pm$, in exact wave-map gauge with the same target $g$, exist on the finite domains needed to reach $\mathcal S_T$, with fixed overlap margins and strict future-outflow artificial boundaries. Set $$H_T^\pm=\widehat{g_T^\pm-g},\qquad
 F_T^\pm=H_T^\pm\mp q_T
 \quad\hbox{on the entry collar}.$$ Then $$\label{prep:entry-estimates}
 \mathfrak J_{r,T}(F_T^\pm)\le A_T^2e^{\eta T},\qquad
 \mathfrak J_{r,T}(F_T^+-F_T^-)\le A_T^3e^{\eta T}.$$ The exact changes, packet, and errors vanish on the earlier needed entry interval $v<(1-\nu_0)T$. In particular, $r=61$ is permitted, with an arbitrarily small prescribed loss. The scaled linear deep residual can simultaneously be at most $e^{-4\kappa T}$ through order $60$, or at any other finite orders fixed in advance. The same statement holds for the separately prepared exchanged branch.

Let $s\ge3$. Let $D_\pm=(\delta+a_\pm,b_\pm)$ be smooth positive data on $\mathbb R^3$ with symbol orders $-1,-2$ at every order, and suppose $$\|D_\pm-(\delta,0)\|_{X^s}\le\varepsilon_s.$$ Suppose the smooth compact sources $\phi_\pm$ are supported in $\{|y'|\ge1\}$ and equal $\Phi(D_\pm)$ there. For sufficiently small $\varepsilon_s$ and $b_2:=\max_\pm\|\phi_\pm\|_{Y^s}$ there are corrections $v_\pm$ supported in $\{|y'|\ge1\}$ such that $$\Phi(D_\pm+v_\pm)=0\quad (|y'|\ge1),\qquad
 \|v_\pm\|_{X^s}\le C_s b_2.$$ They are obtained with the same $\mathcal K$ and satisfy $$\label{prep:abstract-odd-correction}
 \|v_+-v_-\|_{X^s}
 \le C_s\left(\|\phi_+-\phi_-\|_{Y^s}
                  +\|D_+-D_-\|_{X^s}b_2\right).$$ Each correction is smooth across the unit sphere and has symbol bounds of orders $-1,-2$ at every derivative order. Only the stated base norms have to be small. No vacuum equation is required for $D_\pm$ in the artificial interior $|y'|<1$.

Fix $0<\delta<\kappa/32$ and the prescribed finite data seminorm. Use Proposition <a href="#bg:packet" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="bg:packet">21</a> and Proposition <a href="#prep:signed-entry" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prep:signed-entry">24</a> with sufficiently high fixed orders, sufficiently small losses and late support parameter, and sufficiently accurate linear residuals. For sufficiently small fixed $\zeta>0$ and sufficiently large $T$, the two entry data have smooth vacuum evolutions $g_T^\pm$ on the strict domain <a href="#energy:domain" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="energy:domain">[energy:domain]</a>, in wave-map gauge with target $g$. Let $H_T^\pm$ be the relative arrays of $g_T^\pm-g$, and put $$F_T^\pm=H_T^\pm\mp q_T,\qquad
 F_T^{\mathrm{odd}}=F_T^+-F_T^-.$$ Then $$\label{energy:conclusion}
 \begin{aligned}
 \lVert F_T^\pm\rVert_{C^{30}_{\mathrm{mix}}(\Omega_T)}
   &\le e^{(\kappa-2\alpha+\delta)T},\\
 \lVert F_T^{\mathrm{odd}}\rVert_{C^2_{\mathrm{mix}}(\Omega_T)}
   &\le e^{(\kappa-3\alpha+\delta)T}
            +e^{(2\kappa-4\alpha+\delta)T}.
 \end{aligned}$$ The exact changes and all three error fields have support in $v\ge(1-\nu_0)T-o(1)$. The time slices remain spacelike and the two moving side faces remain strict future exits. Moreover, for some $c>0$, $$\label{energy:small-relative}
 \lVert H_T^\pm\rVert_{C^{30}_{\mathrm{mix}}}
       \le e^{-(\kappa/2+c)T},\qquad
 e^{o(T)}\left(\lVert F_T^{\mathrm{odd}}\rVert_{C^2_{\mathrm{mix}}}
          +\sum_\pm\lVert H_T^\pm\rVert_{C^2_{\mathrm{mix}}}^2\right)
       =o(A_T).$$ Here every subexponential factor is at a fixed finite order. No higher seminorm of the fixed smooth background is required to be small.

For levels $0,\ldots,K$ satisfying <a href="#energy:hierarchy" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="energy:hierarchy">[energy:hierarchy]</a>, with fixed $K$, there is a subexponential $P_T$, uniform for all $0\le s\le t\le L$, such that $$\label{energy:propagator}
 \sum_{j\le K}E_j(t)
 \le P_T\left\{
 e^{\mathcal R_T(t)-\mathcal R_T(s)}\sum_{j\le K}E_j(s)
 +\int_s^t e^{\mathcal R_T(t)-\mathcal R_T(\tau)}
                       \sum_{j\le K}S_j(\tau)\,d\tau\right\}.$$

On the fixed canonical tube of Lemma <a href="#attach:canonical-tube" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="attach:canonical-tube">31</a>, intersected with the actual deep domain, the new unit shell has a simple root $z=z_T^\pm(v,Y)$ close to $z_0(v,Y)$, with $$\label{attach:root-bound}
 \lVert z_T^\pm-z_0\rVert_{C^2_Y}
 \le \varepsilon_T^{\mathrm{sh}},\qquad
 \varepsilon_T^{\mathrm{sh}}=\varepsilon_T e^{o(T)}.$$ The associated canonical vector field satisfies $|V_T^\pm-V_0|\le C\varepsilon_T^{\mathrm{sh}}$. On this root $dv/d\tau>0$. The estimates concern $\pi_v$ itself.

At $p_T$ define $$\label{holo:scaled-curvature}
 C_T^\pm=a_p^2\operatorname{Riem}(g_T^\pm)
       (E_1^\pm,E_2^\pm,E_1^\pm,E_2^\pm).$$ There is a smooth function $\mathcal C_T$ of the relative metric array and its ordinary jets through order two such that $C_T^\pm=\mathcal C_T(H_T^\pm)$. On a fixed small neighborhood of the zero relative array its first two derivatives have norm at most $e^{o(T)}$. Consequently $$\label{holo:signed-taylor}
 C_T^+-C_T^-=2D\mathcal C_T(0)[q_T]+o(A_T).$$

For sufficiently large $T$, every loop in Equation <a href="#holo:surface" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="holo:surface">[holo:surface]</a> is defined inside the attached future observation box. There is $Q_T=e^{o(T)}$ such that $$\label{holo:loop-estimates}
 \mathcal L_T(h)\leq a_phQ_T,\qquad
 \sup_{0\leq h\leq17h_T}\|\mathcal H_T^{(18)}(h)\|\leq Q_T.$$ Moreover, $$\label{holo:second-coefficient}
 \mathcal H_T(0)=\operatorname{Id},\qquad
 \frac12\mathcal H_T''(0)=\pm a_p^2R^{g'}(E_1,E_2)(p_T)$$ as endomorphism matrices in the based frame.
